members of the Enterobacteriales; however, they are

only very rarely annotated as being phage related, much
less as prophages [38]. Often a genome may contain an
integration hot spot such as that found for Lactococcus
lactis subsp. cremoris which contains 20% of its genome
as IS elements [39]. This suggests that a genome can
exist in an active evolutionary state, as it can readily
accommodate new DNA and/or loose genome regions

